{erl_opts, [debug_info]}.

%% Deliberately NO opentelemetry / cowboy_telemetry dependency here, and no
%% Elixir/Mix anywhere in this project. This app represents a production
%% Cowboy service whose owners never added observability and never touched
%% Elixir. Unlike vanilla_app (Phoenix), plain Cowboy does not emit
%% :telemetry events on its own, so this app's own listener config carries
%% NO stream_handlers override either — proving the spike must retrofit
%% cowboy_telemetry into an already-running listener from entirely outside,
%% not just rely on an app-supplied hook.
{deps, []}.

%% NOTE: this app is NOT actually built with `rebar3 release`. Two
%% independent problems ruled that out in this environment:
%%   1. rebar3's own hex client couldn't resolve cowboy/cowlib here (a
%%      proxy quirk, unrelated to the spike) — worked around by symlinking
%%      the already-fetched sources from ../otel_bundle/deps/ under
%%      _checkouts/, which rebar3 does pick up for compiling.
%%   2. relx then failed with "Application needed for release not found:
%%      cowboy" even with the checkout compiled — not diagnosed further,
%%      since (2) made the whole rebar3-release path a dead end regardless
%%      of (1).
%% run_spike_erlang.sh builds the actual release by hand instead: erlc for
%% this app's own modules, a hand-written vanilla_app_erlang.rel, and
%% systools:make_script/2 for the boot script — using the ALREADY-COMPILED
%% cowboy/cowlib/ranch that `mix compile` produced under
%% ../otel_bundle/_build/prod/lib/. That path is real OTP release tooling
%% (systools is what relx itself calls under the hood), just without relx
%% or rebar3 in the loop, so it's no less representative of a real release
%% boot for what this spike is testing.
